WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.195
Operational technology, usually shortened to OT,

00:00:03.195 --> 00:00:07.200
is the collection of hardware and software used to monitor or control physical processes.

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:10.605
OT reads conditions in the real world, makes control decisions,

00:00:10.605 --> 00:00:14.695
and causes equipment to act. A temperature sensor, a controller that opens a valve,

00:00:14.695 --> 00:00:18.350
and the workstation that shows an operator the process state can all belong to the same OT

00:00:18.350 --> 00:00:23.255
environment. OT is present wherever digital systems interact with physical operations.

00:00:23.255 --> 00:00:26.845
Manufacturing plants use it to run production lines and industrial robots.

00:00:26.845 --> 00:00:30.300
Electric utilities use it to monitor substations and balance power.

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:34.045
Water facilities control pumps, treatment stages, and chemical dosing.

00:00:34.045 --> 00:00:36.585
Transportation systems manage signaling and traffic.

00:00:36.585 --> 00:00:39.720
Building automation controls heating, ventilation, access, lighting,

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:41.235
and life-safety support systems.

00:00:41.235 --> 00:00:45.275
This physical connection distinguishes OT from a conventional information system.

00:00:45.275 --> 00:00:49.200
A business application primarily stores, processes, or communicates data.

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:53.175
An OT system may do those things, but its purpose is to influence a process.

00:00:53.175 --> 00:00:56.200
Incorrect data in an office report can cause a business problem.

00:00:56.200 --> 00:01:00.215
An incorrect control value can stop machinery, damage equipment, release material,

00:01:00.215 --> 00:01:04.695
or create unsafe conditions. OT environments are therefore cyber-physical systems.

00:01:04.695 --> 00:01:07.905
Digital components, networks, software, operators, machinery,

00:01:07.905 --> 00:01:10.625
and the underlying process must be considered together.

00:01:10.625 --> 00:01:14.615
Security cannot be evaluated solely by looking at an operating system version or firewall

00:01:14.615 --> 00:01:18.700
rule. Analysts must understand what the system controls, how operators use it,

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:20.270
what safe operation requires,

00:01:20.270 --> 00:01:23.400
and what happens when communications or control functions are disrupted.

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:26.700
OT security protects the dependable operation of these systems.

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:30.655
That includes preventing unauthorized changes, detecting abnormal behavior,

00:01:30.655 --> 00:01:34.170
preserving reliable visibility for operators, and supporting safe recovery.

00:01:34.170 --> 00:01:37.595
The central question is not only, “Is the data protected?” It is also,

00:01:37.595 --> 00:01:40.840
“Can the physical operation continue safely and as intended?”
